@model IEnumerable<GymMembers.Models.GymMember>

<h1 class="mb-4">Entity Framework Code First Demo - Gym Members and Classes</h1>

<hr />

<h3>Gym Members</h3>

<div id="members" style="color: red;">
    Loading members...<br />
    The Azure SQL DB is free-tier and so likely needs "waking-up".<br />
    It may take a moment to load the data...
</div>

<hr />


<p>
    This is a C# MVC project that creates a SQL Database via <b>Code First EntityFramework Database Migration</b>, populates it and renders its data.<br />
    It also demonstrates deployment to Azure via Infrastructure as Code (IaC) using  <b>Terraform </b>
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Git repository hosted at: <a href="https://github.com/wizzard262/EntityFrameworkDotNetCoreMvcCodeFirstSqlGymMembership">https://github.com/wizzard262/EntityFrameworkDotNetCoreMvcCodeFirstSqlGymMembership</a></li>
</ul>

<h3>Code First EntityFramework Database Migration</h3>
<ul>
    <li><b>GymMember</b> and <b>ClassSession</b> Data Models are defined.</li>
    <li>The <b>DbContext</b> defines a many-to-many relationship for: <i>A <b>GymMember</b> may attend many <b>ClassSession</b></i></li>
    <li>
        Running the <b>EntityFrameworkDatabaseMigration</b> creates:
        <ul>
            <li>Database <b>GymMembership</b></li>
            <li>Database tables <b>GymMember</b> and <b>ClassSession</b></li>
            <li>The implied many-to-many joining table <b>GymMemberClassSession</b></li>
            <li>Test data, incuding a <b>GymMember</b> <i>(Alice Johnson)</i> attending several <b>ClassSession</b> <i>(Morning Yoga & HIIT Blast)</i></li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<h3>Terraform Deployment to Azure</h3>
<p>
    See Terraform configuration file at: <a href="https://github.com/wizzard262/EntityFrameworkDotNetCoreMvcCodeFirstSqlGymMembership/tree/main/GymMembers/infra">https://github.com/wizzard262/EntityFrameworkDotNetCoreMvcCodeFirstSqlGymMembership/tree/main/GymMembers/infra</a>
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Uses the existing Resource Group <b>ResourceGroup1</b></li>
    <li>
        Creates a new App Service Plan (it could not use any existing plan): <b>asp-gym-membership-b1</b> <br />
        <b>N.B. Terraform cannot create Free-Tier App Service Plan. This was downgradable to Free via Azure Portal</b>
    </li>
    <li>
        Creates a new Azure SQL Database: <b>GymMembership</b> on an existing Azure Sql Server: <b>wizzard262</b> <br />
        <b>N.B. Terraform cannot create Free-Tier DB. This had to be added via Azure Portal</b>
    </li>
    <li>Creates a new Azure App Service (Web App) <b>gym-membership-webapp</b></li>
    <li>Injects a ConnectionString into the App Service named: "<b>DefaultConnection"</b> pointing at the new Azure SQL Database: <b>GymMembership</b></li>
    <li>Sets application settings for the Web App: <b>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT = "Development"</b></li>
</ul>

<h3>Azure DevOps Deployment</h3>
Terraform creates Resources but a deployment Pipeline is still needed to deploy the resources:<a href="#"></a>
<ul>
    <li>C# MVC code to Azure App Service (Web App) <b>gym-membership-webapp</b></li>
    <li>Database Migrations (schema and data) to the Azure SQL Database: <b>GymMembership</b></li>
    <li>Azure DevOps PIpeline used</li>
</ul>

<script>
    // function to wake the database (if it's cold) by making a simple request to the server
    async function wakeDatabase() {
        try {
            await fetch("/Home/WakeDatabase");
        } catch (e) {
            console.error("Wake DB failed", e);
        }
    }
    // function to fetch the members table HTML from the server and insert it into the page
    async function loadMembers() {
        try {
            const response = await fetch("/Home/GetMembers");
            const html = await response.text();
            document.getElementById("members").innerHTML = html;
        } catch (e) {
            document.getElementById("members").innerHTML = "Failed to load members.";
        }
    }

    // this event listener waits for the page to load, then wakes the database and loads the members table
    window.addEventListener("load", async () => {
        // Step 1: Wake the DB (slow if cold)
        await wakeDatabase();

        // Step 2: Load the table (fast once DB is awake)
        await loadMembers();
    });
</script>
